1. Introduction to Docker

Interview:
Docker is a containerization platform that uses a client-server architecture where the Docker daemon manages images and containers, allowing applications to run consistently
across different environments.

Introduction to Docker

Docker is an open-source platform that allows you to package applications and their dependencies into lightweight containers so they can run consistently across any environment.

In simple terms:

Docker ensures your application runs the same on your laptop, server, or cloud.

Docker Vs Virtual Machine

The fundamental difference is how they isolate software: Virtual Machines (VMs) virtualize the hardware, while Docker virtualizes the Operating System (OS). 
A VM includes a full copy of an OS, which makes it heavy and slow to start. Docker containers share the host's OS kernel, making them lightweight and able to start in seconds.

Core Differences
Isolation Level:  VMs: Highly secure with hard boundaries; each has its own kernel. Docker: Process-level isolation; containers share the host kernel.

Resource Usage:  VMs: Heavy; requires GBs of space and pre-allocated RAM.  Docker: Light; images are typically MBs and use RAM on-demand.

Boot Speed:  VMs: Minutes (must boot a full OS).  Docker: Milliseconds to seconds (app starts like a standard process).

Guest OS Support:  VMs: Can run any OS (e.g., Windows on Mac, Linux on Windows).Docker: Limited to the host's kernel type (mostly Linux-based).

2. Docker Architecture:

Docker follows a client-server architecture:

🔹 1. Docker Client
You interact using commands (docker run, docker build)
Sends requests to Docker daemon

🔹 2. Docker Daemon (dockerd)
Core engine that runs on host machine
Builds, runs, and manages containers

🔹 3. Docker Images
Read-only templates used to create containers
Example: Ubuntu, Python images

🔹 4. Docker Containers
Running instances of images
Lightweight and isolated environments

🔹 5. Docker Registry
Stores images (public/private)
Example: Docker Hub

How Docker Works
Docker Client → Docker Daemon → Images → Containers → Registry


3. Installing Docker (Linux / Ubuntu)

Step 1: Update system
sudo apt update

Step 2: Install prerequisites
sudo apt install ca-certificates curl gnupg

Step 3: Add Docker GPG key
sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings

Step 4: Install Docker
sudo apt install docker.io

Step 5: Start Docker
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker

Step 6: Verify installation
docker --version

First Test Command
docker run hello-world

If successful, Docker is working correctly 🎉

4. Installing docker on local terminal

macOS
Download: Choose the Intel or Apple Silicon version based on your Mac's chip.
Install: Open the .dmg file and drag Docker to your Applications folder.
Launch: Open Docker from your Applications to start the engine.
Verify: Open Terminal and type:docker --version


Commands:
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$ sudo systemctl status docker    #This command shows the docker architecture - (dockerd running, contains containerd)Docker Application Container Engine
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$ docker                          #shows all docker commands
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$ docker ps                        # does not give permission to see as ubuntu is not permitted
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$ whoami
ubuntu
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$ sudo usermod -aG docker $USER    #command is used to add us/current user/ubuntu into user mode of docker to access docker
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$ docker ps
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$                                   # still permission is denied so refresh 
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$ newgrp docker
ubuntu@ip-172-31-87-248:~$docker ps
no error

